{
  /*
    `about` and `description` (plan 078) are our own words, facts only,
    from these public-domain sources; "n" is the archive.org BookReader
    index, archive.org/details/<id>/page/n<N>.

    primum-mobile, zodiac, olam-yesodot: their names and glosses, Mathers,
      The Kabbalah Unveiled, 1887, Introduction §53, archive.org b24884443,
      pp. 27–28 (n40–n41). Their places (the Primum Mobile outermost, the
      fixed stars beyond the planets, the elements below the Moon) are the
      common Ptolemaic order of the spheres, and mazal is everyday Hebrew
      for luck; neither is taken from a particular source.
    earth: The Equinox I(2), 1909, Liber O and "The Temple of Solomon the
      King", archive.org crowley-equinox_202110, file "The Equinox Vol. 1,
      No. 2", pp. 22, 242; Mathers, as above, for the sphere of the elements.
    uranus: Herschel, "Account of a Comet", Phil. Trans. 71 (1781) 492,
      doi:10.1098/rstl.1781.0056; Bode, Von dem neu entdeckten Planeten,
      1784, archive.org bub_gb_ZqA5AAAAcAAJ, pp. 95–96 (n98–n99); Wikimedia
      Commons File:Uranus_symbol.svg and File:Uranus_monogram.svg; Leo,
      below.
    neptune: MNRAS 7 (1846) 153, doi:10.1093/mnras/7.9.153; Encke,
      Astronomische Nachrichten 25 (1847) 49, doi:10.1002/asna.18470250402;
      Leo, below.
    uranus and neptune, the modern rulerships and that they were unsettled:
      Leo, Astrology for All, 1899, ch. XV, archive.org bub_gb_8CwSAAAAYAAJ;
      The Art of Synthesis, 1st ed. 1912 (archive.org artofsynthesis00leoa,
      a 1926 copy), pp. 100, 117 (n127, n144); How to Judge a Nativity,
      archive.org howtojudgenativi00leoa, pp. 27, 123, 126 (n50, n146, n149).
    rahu: Wilson, A Complete Dictionary of Astrology, 1819, archive.org
      b29298180, pp. 13, 83 (n44, n114); Lilly, Christian Astrology, 1647,
      archive.org b30338724, p. 83 (n116); Monier-Williams, A
      Sanskrit-English Dictionary, 1899, archive.org
      ASanskritEnglishDictionary, p. 879 (n920).
    ketu: Wilson and Lilly, as above; Monier-Williams, as above, p. 309
      (n350).

    `intelligenceId` and `spiritId` of the seven planets: the intelligences
    and spirits Agrippa names with the planets' magic squares, De occulta
    philosophia (Cologne, 1533), Book II, chapter 22, the "tables of the
    planets", archive.org DeOccultaPhilosophiaLoc1533, pp. cxlviii–cxlix
    (n163, n164). Six intelligences and all seven spirits are his, up to
    spelling. Agrippa gives the Moon no intelligence of its own: in its
    place is an "intelligentia intelligentiarum", which is
    `intelligenceOfIntelligences` (n164). Its sources:
      agrippa1533: the name and its Hebrew, as above, numbered 3321, the
        sum of the Moon's square; the Hebrew adds up to 3321 with final
        mem counted as 600.
      The English of 1651, Three Books of Occult Philosophy, p. 243,
        calls it "The Intelligency of the Intelligence of the Moon" and
        misprints the name; the line is cut off in archive.org b30335231
        (n256) and legible in
        bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_three-books-of-occult-ph_agrippa-henricus-cornel_1651
        (n271).
      crowley777: Crowley, 777 Revised (London, 1955), Table III, col.
        CXCIV, line 13, the transliteration of col. LXXVIII, "Intelligences
        of the Planets", read in Celephaïs Press's electronic edition,
        archive.org aleister-crowley-liber-777, p. 20 (n27). We have not
        seen a scan of the first edition (1909).
      regardie1940: the Golden Dawn's papers, Regardie, The Golden Dawn,
        vol. 4 (Chicago, 1940), p. 86, archive.org
        IsraelRegardie-TheGoldenDawn-Vol4-1940 (n80), "Intelligence of the
        Intelligences of the Moon".
    777's and Regardie's are variants of the name in their own spellings,
    not romanisations of Agrippa's Hebrew: 777's final mems are counted
    as 700 (Crowley's note to col. LXXIX) and its "va A'ad" reads ועד for
    Agrippa's עד; Regardie, who prints the name in capitals, ends it in a
    plural "Ruachoth" and another last word, without "be-".
    `intelligenceId`'s Shelachel, for the Moon, is a name found in
    present-day sources. Its origin is not known to us: we have not found
    it in Agrippa (1533 or 1651), in 777 (the revised edition of 1955),
    in Regardie's fourth volume or in Heydon, Theomagia (1663/4),
    archive.org theomagiaortempl00heyd.
   */

